TI  - Tracheostomy Update: When and How.
PG  - 311-322
LID - S0749-0704(16)30119-1 [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.ccc.2016.12.007 [doi]
AB  - Tracheostomy remains one of the most commonly performed surgical procedures in 
      the setting of acute respiratory failure. Tracheostomy literature focuses on 2 
      aspects of this procedure: when (timing) and how (technique). Recent trials have 
      failed to demonstrate an effect of tracheostomy timing on most clinically 
      important endpoints. Nonetheless, relative to continued translaryngeal 
      intubation, studies suggest that tracheostomy use is associated with less need 
      for sedation and enhanced patient comfort. Evidence likewise suggests that 
      percutaneous dilational tracheostomy is advantageous with respect to cost and 
      complication profile and should be considered the preferred approach in 
      appropriately selected patients.
