IS  - 1735-0344 (Linking)
VI  - 19
IP  - 1
DP  - 2020 Jan
TI  - Performing Percutaneous Dilational Tracheostomy without using Fiberoptic 
      Bronchoscope.
PG  - 60-65
AB  - BACKGROUND: Percutaneous tracheostomy is an elective method that is increasingly 
      being taken up in the intensive care unit alongside the patient's bed. In many 
      centers, bronchoscopy is used, but the necessity of using bronchoscopy in 
      percutaneous tracheostomy has not yet been determined. Discontinuing use of 
      bronchoscopy can potentially reduce the cost and increase the efficiency of 
      percutaneous tracheostomy. Therefore, in this study, we performed a percutaneous 
      dilatational tracheostomy without using fiberoptic bronchoscopy. MATERIALS AND 
      METHODS: This study was performed as a descriptive epidemiological survey among 
      70 patients in Shahid Rajaei Hospital of Qazvin in 2015 and 2016. The results 
      were assessed in the patients. RESULTS: In this study, pneumothorax, trauma, 
      major and minor bleeding, cuff leak and change to surgical procedures as well as 
      accidental extubation were not seen. However, subcutaneous emphysema, 
      mal-position and hypoxia each were seen in one patient (1.4%). CONCLUSION: 
      Totally the results demonstrated that percutaneous dilatation tracheostomy 
      without fiberoptic bronchoscopic guidance is useful and safe.
